<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Lima culture</h1><p class="st-meta">100 CE – 650 CE (approx.) · capital Maranga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A culture of the central coast of Peru whose adobe pyramids and irrigation canals laid the foundations of settlement in the valleys where Lima now stands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> culture took shape around 100 CE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chillon-river">Chillón</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimac-river">Rímac</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurin-river">Lurín</a> valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru's</a> central coast. Communities built canals that turned the desert valleys into farmland and began raising large adobe platform mounds.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its main centre was the Maranga complex in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimac-river">Rímac</a> valley, with large mounds such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-san-marcos">Huaca San Marcos</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huaca-pucllana">Huaca Pucllana</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miraflores-lima">Miraflores</a> was built of small handmade adobe bricks set like books on a shelf. Society was organised around ceremonial centres led by local elites. The economy combined irrigation farming with fishing. Pottery shows an interlocking serpent and fish design. The early phase of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lurin-river">Lurín</a> valley also belongs to this culture.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From around 650 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> culture declined as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/wari-empire">Wari Empire</a> extended its influence along the coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacamac">Pachacamac</a> continued as a sacred site and later became the centre of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ychsma">Ychsma</a>. Many Lima mounds survive among the modern city's streets.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lima-culture.jpg" alt="Lima culture" width="258" height="367"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_cultura_lima.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 CE – 650 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Maranga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Unknown local cults</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maranga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=375&amp;focus=lima-culture" aria-label="Lima culture on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2561 2628 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lima culture lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2881 2884L2868 2783"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2868 2783h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2881 2884h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2881 2884h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Maranga. <a href="/atlas?year=375&amp;focus=lima-culture">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

